# Routes the server mounts that the web client does not call.
#
# Each is here with the caller it exists for. A route with no caller at all
# belongs deleted, not listed: this file is for the ones whose caller is
# something other than the shipped interface.
#
# Unlike routes.allow, a line here does not fail the check either way. The
# reverse direction is reported and never fatal, because the routes a sync
# client or an operator calls are not ones the web interface has a screen for,
# and a check that failed on them is one people learn to silence.

# The container runtime's health probe, which has no credential and no screen.
/api/v1/system/health

# The change channel. The client opens it as a WebSocket rather than through
# the request helper, so it is a socket the check cannot see as a call.
/api/v1/events

# The browser's own navigation, not a call the client makes: signing in through
# a provider is a redirect out and a redirect back.
/api/v1/auth/oidc/start
/api/v1/auth/oidc/callback

# Public share links. A visitor follows the URL; nothing in the interface
# fetches these.
/s/{token}
/s/{token}/download

# The resumable upload protocol's discovery request. A client sends it to learn
# which version to speak, and this client already knows.
/api/v1/uploads
/api/v1/uploads/{id}

# The second spelling of a route the client calls under one verb. Both are
# mounted because other callers send the other one, and dropping either turns a
# working request into "method not allowed".
/api/v1/files/write
/api/v1/jobs/{id}/cancel
/api/v1/links
/api/v1/links/{id}

# The SMB apply. There is no button for it in the shipped interface, and there
# does not need to be one now: every write that changes what SMB should serve
# republishes on its own. It stays mounted as the operator's apply-now, for
# forcing a push after editing the configuration file by hand.
/api/v1/admin/smb/apply
